    hgTitle: "Senior Nutrition Program Operating Model Shift and Meal Service Procurement",
    hgText:
      "The City of San Bruno held a study session to review the Senior Nutrition and Transportation Programs, including alternative models for discussion and direction, because the City is approaching a decision point on whether to enter into year 1 of a new four-year grant cycle with San Mateo County or move to an alternative City-operated model. The staff report states that the Senior Center has operated these programs since 2004 under a County grant-funded model, but that costs are rising and the City projects the General Fund annual subsidy will exceed $750,000 by the end of the grant cycle in 2030. For FY 2026-27, the County grant reimbursement for both programs is estimated at a not-to-exceed $198,491, while total expenditures are projected to exceed $800,000.\n\nA future contracting opportunity is most clearly indicated under the independent City-funded alternatives. Under Model 2, the City would opt out of the four-year County grant commitment and instead operate the programs independently, without County funding or oversight. The report expressly notes that this would give the City greater control over program structure, fees, eligibility, resident and non-resident participation, menus, staffing, branding, and service delivery. It also specifically contemplates flexibility during the upcoming Senior Center renovation closure, including boxed meal options, alternate meal locations, multiple community-based sites, and other service delivery models. Those elements indicate possible future purchases or service contracts relating to meal catering, food service operations, boxed meal fulfillment, temporary alternate-site operations, and program support during renovation.\n\nThe report provides detailed program volumes and cost information that could support a future solicitation. In FY 2025-26, the program averaged approximately 2,084 meals per month, totaling just over 25,000 meals annually. The fiscal table for FY 2026-27 shows annual catered meals cost of $325,104 under Model 1 and Model 2, plus $200,064 for produce and miscellaneous supplies. Under Model 2, the total expenditure is projected at $696,328 with a General Fund subsidy of approximately $374,051. The report also notes the City incurs approximately $8 per meal in supplemental costs including milk, fresh produce, and other supplies. Since the current and alternative models rely on catered meals and related supplies, food service vendors, commissary operators, senior meal specialists, or nonprofit nutrition partners could potentially be relevant if the City moves away from the County structure.\n\nThe report further states that staff seeks Council feedback on how the programs should be structured during the upcoming Senior Center renovation closure, and whether affordability protections such as sponsorships, partnerships, a dedicated assistance fund, or a free/reduced meal option should be built into a fee-based model. This is not yet an award, but it is a concrete policy discussion of future service delivery and alternative operating arrangements that could lead to contracted meal services or related program support.",
