      "The University of Wisconsin System Administration requests that the Board of Regents recommend this project of $16,305,000 Program Revenue Supported Borrowing to selectively reconfigure and renovate the University Union dining, kitchen, and serving areas at UW-Green Bay.\n\nThis project reconfigures the dining, kitchen, and serving areas to improve operational efficiencies, functionality, and accessibility. Cross traffic will be eliminated from the entry and dish return areas. The serving counter, casework, flooring surfaces, and bulkhead will be removed. All building infrastructure systems, food service equipment, and furnishings will be replaced and renovated to serve the resulting operational layout. Air supply and exhaust, electrical, lighting, telecommunication, domestic water, and natural gas utility distribution and connection points will be reconfigured to accommodate the new equipment positions and operational layout.\n\nThe undersized walk-in coolers and freezers will be replaced with appropriately sized and energy-efficient units. The dry storage area will be expanded and the food preparation and cook's line will be reconfigured to meet current and future dining program needs. New serving concepts, a beverage counter, and a salad bar will be introduced and incorporated into the final design solution. All architectural finishes will be replaced, and seating capacity will be increased to approximately 650 users.\n\nThe background notes that UW-Green Bay's dining program expanded from 750 meal plans in 2004 to almost 2,000 in 2026, and that this success has outpaced the capacity and efficiency of the current Catering and Bakery kitchen, last updated in 1997. The current facility is described as undersized and outdated, with no food preparation area, operational bottlenecks, inadequate storage, and frequent maintenance issues caused by failing infrastructure. The Marketplace serving and dining areas also require comprehensive renovation to align with the current meal service model, improve sightlines and traffic flow, and support faster student processing through new self-check-in gates.\n\nThe project also notes pending approval for a segregated fee rate increase estimated to occur in Fall 2030. Fee impacts are explicitly tied to this project, with proposed incremental increases of $50 in 2030-31, 2031-32, and 2032-33, for a total increase of $150.\n\nThe schedule shows A/E selection in July 2027, Design Report (75%) in August 2028, approval in December 2028, bid opening in February 2029, start in May 2029, substantial completion in August 2030, and close out in February 2031. This is a strong future procurement for food service design, kitchen equipment, MEP reconfiguration, refrigeration, interiors, furnishings, and construction services.",
